\section{Approach}

Traditional approaches to narrative consistency often fail due to the "context window bottleneck." We introduce a novel architecture that leverages \textbf{State-Space Models (SSMs)} to achieve practically infinite context retention.

\subsection{1. Entity-Centric World State}
Replace the single global state with entity-partitioned states:
\[ \text{WorldState} = \{ \text{global\_state, entity\_states: } \{\text{entity\_id} \to \text{BDH\_state}\} \} \]

It Detects entities during ingestion. Route token updates into relevant entity states. Allow shared updates between global and entity-specific states. Keep BDH as the core state accumulator.

\subsection{2. Temporal Conditioning \& State Transitions}
Introduce explicit temporal grounding: Encoding chapter index, scene index or token timestep. Modify the BDH delta rule to include temporal gates. 
\[ S_t = \alpha S_{t-1} + g(\text{entity, time, token}) \odot (K_t^T V_t) \]
It learns when information overwrites vs coexists. It supports role changes over time.

\subsection{3. Causal Gating in State Updates}
Extend inference beyond Siamese similarity:
For each statement, generate the statement and its explicit negation.
Encode both conditioned on the world state and measure state conflict, surprise and overwrite pressure. This is used as an energy-based signal for contradiction detection.

\subsection{4. Counterfactual Consistency Checking}
This is the "Brain" of classification logic. It avoids training a supervised classifier on millions of examples. Instead, it uses an Energy-Based Model (EBM) approach.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Input}: A statement $S$ (e.g., "I have never been to Paris") and a WorldState.
    \item \textbf{Surprise Computation}:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The model encodes $S$ into the current WorldState.
        \item It measures the State Update Magnitude ($\Delta S$). If the statement forces the model to drastically change its internal state, it means the statement was "unexpected" or "new info" (High Surprise/High Energy).
        \item If the statement aligns with known facts, the state update is minimal (Low Surprise/Low Energy).
    \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Decision}:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Calculate Energy $E(S)$ and $E(\neg S)$.
        \item \textbf{Contradiction}: $E(S) > E(\neg S)$. The statement matches the world state worse than its opposite.
        \item \textbf{Consistent}: $E(S) < E(\neg S)$. The statement matches the world state better than its opposite.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\subsection{5. Multi-Task Training Signals}
In addition to consistent vs contradict, introduce auxiliary heads for entity relevance, temporal validity, overwrite vs coexist decision. Even weak supervision is acceptable.

\subsection{6. Entity-Aware World State}
The critical insight here is that a single global state for a whole book is too noisy.
\textbf{Logic}: WorldState Class: Container that holds one \texttt{global\_state} (general narrative) and a dictionary of \texttt{entity\_states} (one per character).

\textbf{Ingestion Flow}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The text is processed chunk by chunk. Every chunk updates the \texttt{global\_state}.
    \item \textbf{Entity Detection}: The system checks which entities are mentioned in the chunk.
    \item \textbf{Gated Entity Update}: Specific state of Entity X in \texttt{entity\_states['X']} is updated. Crucially, this update is gated by an \textbf{EntityWriteGate}. This gate calculates a "novelty score" (based on variance difference) to decide how much this new chunk should modify the character's long-term memory. This prevents irrelevant mentions from extracting too much "write energy".
    \item \textbf{Inference Merging (AdaptiveMerge)}: When querying for a character, the system doesn't just use the entity state. It uses an MLP to learn an alpha weight to blend the \texttt{global\_state} (context) and \texttt{entity\_state} (specifics): $S_{\text{final}} = \alpha S_{\text{global}} + (1-\alpha) S_{\text{entity}}$.
\end{itemize}

