\subsection{7. Detailed Data Flow}

\textbf{A. Initialization \& Training Phase}
\\State Building (The "Reading" Phase): The \texttt{NarrativeConsistencySystem} iterates through the book text. It feeds tokens into the BDH model. It routes state updates to \texttt{global\_state} and relevant \texttt{entity\_states}.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Output}: A saved WorldState object for each book, effectively a "compressed neural representation" of the entire book's plot and character arcs.
    \item \textbf{Sentence Analyzer} computes granular scores (temporal, causal, thematic) using a \textbf{CoherenceClassifier} (a standard MLP classifier), though the primary "truth" signal comes from the BDH Energy method.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{B. Inference Phase (Per User Query)}
\\Input: A (Book, Character, Statement) triplet.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{State Retrieval}: The system looks up the pre-computed WorldState for the Book.
    \item \textbf{Context Blending}: It retrieves the specific \texttt{entity\_state} for the Character and blends it with the \texttt{global\_state} using AdaptiveMerge.
    \item \textbf{Consistency Check}:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item It runs the Counterfactual Checker on the Statement vs. the Blended State.
        \item It computes the "Conflict Ratio" (Ratio of Surprise).
        \item \textbf{Final Output}: Returns "Consistent" or "Contradict" based on which version (original or negated) had lower surprisingness.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\section{Handling Infinite Context}

Our system addresses long-context narrative consistency by replacing the standard Transformer attention mechanism with a state-space recurrent memory model, implemented through the BDH (Baby Dragon Hatchling) architecture. This enables effectively unbounded context processing while keeping memory usage constant. Unlike standard Transformers (e.g., BERT, GPT), which rely on growing attention caches, our approach maintains a compressed world state that evolves incrementally as the narrative progresses.

In a standard Transformer, each new token attends to all previous tokens, Memory grows as $O(T)$ with sequence length. BDH maintains a persistent latent state that summarizes all prior text seen so far.

\subsection{1. Core Idea: Infinite Context via Fixed-Size State}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Memory size: $O(1)$ (constant), Context length: unbounded, No KV cache, No quadratic attention. 
    \item The model reads from the existing internal state. Updates that state using the new tokens. The internal state acts as a compressed narrative memory. This gives full control over the memory lifecycle, which Transformers do not.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{2. Entity-Aware Long-Term Memory}
During ingestion, the global state is always updated. Entity states are selectively updated if mentioned. This allows precise backstory tracking, clean separation of character facts, stronger consistency checking.

\subsection{3. Gated State Updates (Preventing Memory Pollution)}
EntityWriteGate: Instead of blindly overwriting memory, you use a rule-based gate this Measures variance difference, Updates entity state only if new information is meaningful:
\[ \text{entity\_state} = \text{old\_state} + \text{gate} \cdot (\text{new\_state} - \text{old\_state}) \]
This ensures Descriptive text $\approx$ no memory change, Important events $\approx$ strong memory update. This is crucial for long novels, where most text is irrelevant to constraints.

\subsection{4. Temporal Stability via Decay}
Long-range reasoning must respect time. You implement temporal decay:
\[ \text{temporal\_decay} = \exp(-\beta \cdot (\text{query\_time} - \text{fact\_time})) \]
This Reduces the influence of very old facts, Prevents stale information from dominating, Improves robustness for long narratives.

\section{Distinguish Causal Signals from Noise}

Variance-Based "Novelty" Gating (EntityWriteGate): If a chunk of text describes a scene that doesn't change the character's status (e.g., descriptions of trees), the variance profile matches the existing state. The gate stays low, treating it as noise. If the character acts or learns something new, the variance spikes, opening the gate to write the signal. It assumes that Noise is repetitive/stable (low variance changes), while Signal is state-changing (high variance changes).
\[ \text{gate} = \text{self.base\_gate} + (1.0 - \text{self.base\_gate}) \cdot \min(\text{novelty\_score} \cdot \text{self.sensitivity}, 1.0) \]

Learnable "Erase/Write" Gates: the model learns to output erase=1 for information that is no longer causally relevant (e.g., "It was raining yesterday" ceases to matter for today's plot), allowing the limited state capacity to focus entirely on remaining causal signals.
\begin{equation}
    S_{new} = \underbrace{(1 - \sigma(g_{erase})) \odot S_{old}}_{\text{Forget Noise}} + \underbrace{\sigma(g_{write}) \odot \tilde{S}_{input}}_{\text{Inject Signal}}
\end{equation}

Entity Isolation (The "Source-Separation" Filter):
It treats other characters' storylines as "noise" relative to the current character.

\section{Limitations}

Despite its theoretical robustness, the system exhibits specific failure modes:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Entity Resolution Failure}: If specific pronouns or nicknames are not correctly linked to the Entity ID during ingestion, the $\mathcal{S}_{entity}$ state remains stale, leading to hallucinations.
    \item \textbf{Temporal Bleed}: While we implement temporal decay, extremely long narratives may still suffer from catastrophic forgetting of early-chapter constraints if the "novelty" threshold is set too high.
\end{enumerate}

